---
import Lesson from '../../layouts/LessonLayout.astro';

const body = `<h1>4.4 — Nested Loops: The GridArchitect &amp; Fog-of-War Build</h1>

<p>
    <strong>CED Topic 2.11 · 90-Minute Unified Build.</strong> When one loop sits inside another,
    the inner loop runs to completion for <em>every single step</em> of the outer loop. Today you
    will build <code>GridArchitect.java</code> — generating bordered ASCII dungeon rooms,
    Manhattan-distance fog-of-war maps, dependent staircase patterns, and unique item-pair
    combinations.
</p>

<div class="callout">
    <strong>Spec-First Workflow (Download Both Files):</strong>
    <ul>
        <li>
            <a href="../data/GridArchitect.java" download>GridArchitect.java</a> — starter stub
            where you will assemble all four nested-loop methods.
        </li>
        <li>
            <a href="../data/GridArchitectCheck.java" download>GridArchitectCheck.java</a> — final
            acceptance verification suite (13 checks across 4 parts).
        </li>
    </ul>
    <p style="margin-top:0.5rem; margin-bottom:0.25rem;">
        <strong>How to work today:</strong> Draw small grids (<code>2&times;2</code>,
        <code>3&times;4</code>, <code>height = 1</code>, <code>maxTier = 1</code>) on scratch paper
        first, draft the loop headers and conditions in the prompts below, assemble each method in
        <code>GridArchitect.java</code>, and print your grids from a scratch <code>main</code> to
        inspect every character and newline <em>before</em> running
        <code>GridArchitectCheck</code> at the end.
    </p>
    <pre><code>javac GridArchitect.java GridArchitectCheck.java
java GridArchitectCheck</code></pre>
</div>

<!-- ============================================================ -->
<div class="activity expand-app">
    <div class="activity-header">
        <span class="activity-label">Parts 1 &amp; 2 · 2D Coordinate Grids</span>
        <span class="activity-time">~45 min</span>
    </div>

    <h3>Part 1: <code>buildBorderedRoom(int rows, int cols)</code></h3>

    <h4>AP Specification</h4>
    <p>
        Return a multi-line <code>String</code> representing a <code>rows</code> by
        <code>cols</code> dungeon room (indices <code>0 .. rows - 1</code> and
        <code>0 .. cols - 1</code>; precondition: <code>rows &gt;= 2</code>, <code>cols &gt;= 2</code>):
    </p>
    <ul>
        <li>Every cell on the outer boundary (first row <code>r == 0</code>, last row
            <code>r == rows - 1</code>, first column <code>c == 0</code>, or last column
            <code>c == cols - 1</code>) is a wall (<code>"#"</code>).</li>
        <li>Every interior cell is floor (<code>"."</code>).</li>
        <li>Append a newline (<code>"\\n"</code>) at the end of <strong>every</strong> row,
            including the final row.</li>
        <li><strong>Worked examples:</strong>
            <ul>
                <li><code>buildBorderedRoom(3, 4)</code> &rarr; <code>"####\\n#..#\\n####\\n"</code></li>
                <li><code>buildBorderedRoom(2, 2)</code> &rarr; <code>"##\\n##\\n"</code> (all four cells are on the border; zero interior cells).</li>
            </ul>
        </li>
    </ul>

    <div class="tinker">
        <h3>Build Step-by-Step: Draft the Pieces for <code>buildBorderedRoom</code></h3>
        <ol>
            <li><strong>Piece A (Grid Loops):</strong> Write the <code>result</code> initialization
                and the nested <code>for</code> loop headers for row <code>r</code> and column
                <code>c</code>?</li>
            <li><strong>Piece B (Border Condition &amp; Newline):</strong> Write the compound boolean
                expression that decides whether cell <code>(r, c)</code> is <code>"#"</code> vs.
                <code>"."</code>, and show exactly where <code>result += "\\n";</code> goes relative
                to the inner and outer loop braces?</li>
        </ol>
        <p>
            Combine Pieces A and B with <code>return result;</code> inside
            <code>buildBorderedRoom</code> in <code>GridArchitect.java</code>.
        </p>
    </div>

    <h3>Part 2: <code>renderFogOfWar(int size, int heroRow, int heroCol, int visionRadius)</code></h3>

    <h4>AP Specification</h4>
    <p>
        Render a <code>size</code> by <code>size</code> grid (rows <code>0 .. size - 1</code>,
        columns <code>0 .. size - 1</code>) around the hero at <code>(heroRow, heroCol)</code>:
    </p>
    <ul>
        <li>The hero's exact tile <code>(r == heroRow &amp;&amp; c == heroCol)</code> is
            <code>"@"</code>.</li>
        <li>Any other tile whose <strong>Manhattan distance</strong> from the hero
            (<code>Math.abs(r - heroRow) + Math.abs(c - heroCol)</code>) is
            <code>&lt;= visionRadius</code> is visible floor (<code>"."</code>).</li>
        <li>All tiles beyond <code>visionRadius</code> are shrouded in fog (<code>"?"</code>).</li>
        <li>Append <code>"\\n"</code> at the end of every row.</li>
        <li><strong>Worked examples:</strong>
            <ul>
                <li><code>renderFogOfWar(5, 2, 2, 1)</code> &rarr; center tile <code>(2,2)</code> is <code>@</code>, 4 adjacent tiles at distance <code>1</code> are <code>.</code>, all others are <code>?</code>.</li>
                <li><code>renderFogOfWar(3, 1, 1, 0)</code> &rarr; <code>"???\\n?@?\\n???\\n"</code> (only the hero is visible when <code>visionRadius == 0</code>).</li>
            </ul>
        </li>
    </ul>

    <div class="socratic">
        <h3>Edge-Case Analysis: Row Breaks, Manhattan Diamonds &amp; Branch Priority</h3>
        <ol>
            <li>What happens to the returned string if you accidentally place
                <code>result += "\\n";</code> <em>inside</em> the inner column loop instead of
                between the end of the inner loop and the end of the outer loop?</li>
            <li>Why does the Manhattan distance condition
                <code>Math.abs(r - heroRow) + Math.abs(c - heroCol) &lt;= 1</code> produce a
                5-tile diamond (up, down, left, right, center) rather than a 3&times;3 square? What
                is the Manhattan distance of a diagonal corner like
                <code>(heroRow - 1, heroCol - 1)</code>?</li>
            <li>At the hero's own tile <code>(heroRow, heroCol)</code>, what is its Manhattan
                distance to itself? Why must the <code>if</code> branch for <code>"@"</code> be
                checked <em>before</em> <code>dist &lt;= visionRadius</code>?</li>
        </ol>
    </div>

    <div class="tinker">
        <h3>Build Step-by-Step: Draft the Pieces for <code>renderFogOfWar</code></h3>
        <ol>
            <li><strong>Piece A (Distance &amp; Tile Classifier):</strong> Inside the nested
                <code>r</code> / <code>c</code> loops, write the Manhattan distance calculation and
                the 3-way <code>if / else if / else</code> in priority order for <code>"@"</code>,
                <code>"."</code>, and <code>"?"</code>?</li>
        </ol>
        <p>
            Wrap Piece A in your <code>0 .. size - 1</code> grid loops with row newlines and
            <code>return result;</code> inside <code>renderFogOfWar</code> in
            <code>GridArchitect.java</code>.
        </p>
    </div>
</div>

<!-- ============================================================ -->
<div class="activity expand-app">
    <div class="activity-header">
        <span class="activity-label">Parts 3 &amp; 4 · Dependent Inner Bounds &amp; Build</span>
        <span class="activity-time">~45 min</span>
    </div>

    <h3>Part 3: <code>buildRightStaircase(int height)</code></h3>

    <h4>AP Specification</h4>
    <p>
        For each row <code>r</code> from <code>1</code> through <code>height</code> inclusive
        (return <code>""</code> if <code>height &lt;= 0</code>), append <code>height - r</code> dots
        (<code>"."</code>) followed by <code>r</code> hashes (<code>"#"</code>) and a newline
        (<code>"\\n"</code>).
    </p>
    <ul>
        <li><strong>Worked examples:</strong>
            <ul>
                <li><code>buildRightStaircase(0)</code> &rarr; <code>""</code></li>
                <li><code>buildRightStaircase(1)</code> &rarr; <code>"#\\n"</code> (<code>0</code> dots, <code>1</code> hash)</li>
                <li><code>buildRightStaircase(4)</code>:
                    <pre><code>...#
..##
.###
####</code></pre>
                </li>
            </ul>
        </li>
    </ul>

    <div class="tinker">
        <h3>Build Step-by-Step: Draft the Pieces for <code>buildRightStaircase</code></h3>
        <ol>
            <li><strong>Piece A (Outer Row Loop &amp; Sequential Inner Loops):</strong> Inside
                <code>for (int r = 1; r &lt;= height; r++)</code>, you need <em>two separate
                consecutive</em> inner loops (not nested inside each other!): one for dots and one
                for hashes. Write both inner <code>for</code> loops, the row newline, and explain
                why <code>height &lt;= 0</code> returns <code>""</code> cleanly?</li>
        </ol>
        <p>
            Assemble <code>buildRightStaircase</code> in <code>GridArchitect.java</code> and verify
            <code>height = 1</code> and <code>height = 4</code>.
        </p>
    </div>

    <h3>Part 4: <code>countLuckyLootPairs(int maxTier)</code></h3>

    <h4>AP Specification</h4>
    <p>
        Two treasure chests are forged with integer tiers <code>a</code> and <code>b</code> between
        <code>1</code> and <code>maxTier</code> inclusive. A pair of <strong>distinct</strong>
        tiers is a "Lucky Pair" if <code>(a + b) % 3 == 0</code>, where order does not matter
        (so <code>(1, 2)</code> and <code>(2, 1)</code> are the <em>same</em> pair and must only
        be counted once, and a tier cannot pair with itself like <code>(3, 3)</code>).
    </p>
    <ul>
        <li><strong>Worked examples:</strong>
            <ul>
                <li><code>maxTier = 1</code> &rarr; <code>0</code> (only tier <code>1</code> exists; cannot pair with itself).</li>
                <li><code>maxTier = 2</code> &rarr; <code>1</code> (only <code>(1, 2)</code>).</li>
                <li><code>maxTier = 4</code> &rarr; <code>2</code> (<code>(1, 2)</code> and <code>(2, 4)</code>).</li>
                <li><code>maxTier = 6</code> &rarr; <code>5</code> (<code>(1, 2), (1, 5), (2, 4), (3, 6), (4, 5)</code>).</li>
            </ul>
        </li>
    </ul>

    <div class="bug-hunt">
        <h3>Edge-Case Analysis: The Self-Pair &amp; Double-Count Bug</h3>
        <p>
            Look at what happens when a student starts both loops at <code>1</code>:
        </p>
        <pre><code>// Naive nested loop — why does this return 5 instead of 2 for maxTier = 4?
int count = 0;
for (int a = 1; a &lt;= maxTier; a++) {
    for (int b = 1; b &lt;= maxTier; b++) {
        if ((a + b) % 3 == 0) {
            count++;
        }
    }
}</code></pre>
        <ol>
            <li>List all 5 pairs <code>(a, b)</code> that the naive loop above counts when
                <code>maxTier = 4</code>. Which ones are self-pairs (<code>a == b</code>), and
                which ones are reversed duplicates of an earlier pair?</li>
            <li>How does starting the inner loop at <code>int b = a + 1;</code> automatically
                prevent <em>both</em> self-pairs and reversed duplicates — and why does it also
                naturally return <code>0</code> when <code>maxTier &lt;= 1</code>?</li>
        </ol>
    </div>

    <div class="tinker">
        <h3>Build Step-by-Step: Draft the Pieces for <code>countLuckyLootPairs</code></h3>
        <ol>
            <li><strong>Piece A (Unique-Pair Loop &amp; Condition):</strong> Write the full
                <code>countLuckyLootPairs</code> body using <code>int b = a + 1;</code> and the
                divisibility check?</li>
        </ol>
        <p>
            Assemble <code>countLuckyLootPairs</code> in <code>GridArchitect.java</code>. Test all
            four methods in a scratch <code>main</code> (checking <code>2&times;2</code>, radius
            <code>0</code>, height <code>1</code>, and <code>maxTier = 1</code>), and
            <strong>only then</strong> run <code>java GridArchitectCheck</code> to verify all 13
            checks pass.
        </p>
    </div>

    <div class="checklist">
        <h3>Done When</h3>
        <ul>
            <li>All four methods in <code>GridArchitect.java</code> were built from your pieces and self-tested before running the check</li>
            <li><code>java GridArchitectCheck</code> reports <strong><code>13 passed, 0 failed</code></strong></li>
            <li>You have completed the reflection and code-piece boxes above and downloaded your <code>.json</code> for Canvas</li>
        </ul>
    </div>
</div>

<div class="resources">
    <h3>Reference Docs</h3>
    <ul>
        <li><a href="../docs/unit-04/nested-loops.html">Nested Loops</a> — outer/inner mechanics, dependent bounds</li>
        <li><a href="../docs/unit-02/math-class.html">Math Class</a> — Math.abs() for Manhattan distance</li>
    </ul>
</div>`;
---
<Lesson id="4.4" title="4.4 Nested Loops: GridArchitect &amp; Fog-of-War Build — AP CS A" root=".." css="../style.css">
  <Fragment set:html={body} />
</Lesson>
